            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'Screen printing at home no longer needs a darkroom, emulsion or an exposure unit. With the xTool Screen Printer kit, a laser makes the stencil: it burns away the coating of a pre-coated screen wherever ink should pass, and then you print by hand with a squeegee on T-shirts, tote bags, paper or wood. For screen printing with a laser you need an xTool laser – on our site the kit fits the P3, the S1 and the F-series machines.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'How does screen printing work, and why is the screen the bottleneck?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Screen printing means printing through a stencil. A fine mesh is stretched on a frame, and part of it is covered by a layer that blocks ink. You put ink on the screen and pull it across with a squeegee: it only passes through the open mesh and transfers the design onto the material. Each colour needs its own stencil, and colours are printed one after another.' ],
                [ 'p', 'The print itself takes a moment. The time goes into preparing the screen the traditional way:' ],
